<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jiaqing Emperor</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1760">1760</a>–<a href="/years/1820">1820</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Yongyan · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-aisin-gioro">House of Aisin Gioro</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Jiaqing Emperor was emperor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing dynasty</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> from 1796 to 1820, succeeding his father, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qianlong-emperor">Qianlong Emperor</a>. He purged the corrupt minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heshen">Heshen</a> and suppressed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/white-lotus-rebellion">White Lotus Rebellion</a>, but faced growing financial strain and piracy along the coast. In 1804 he invested <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gia-long">Gia Long</a> as king and granted his country the name Việt Nam.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jiaqing-emperor.jpg" alt="Jiaqing Emperor" width="320" height="529" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Annonymous Qing Dynasty Court Painter · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E6%B8%85_%E4%BD%9A%E5%90%8D_%E3%80%8A%E6%B8%85%E4%BB%81%E5%AE%97%E5%98%89%E5%BA%86%E7%9A%87%E5%B8%9D%E6%9C%9D%E6%9C%8D%E5%83%8F%E3%80%8B.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>The Jiaqing Emperor was born Yongyan on 13 November 1760 at the Old Summer Palace, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>, the 15th son of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qianlong-emperor">Qianlong Emperor</a>. His mother was Noble Consort Ling, daughter of an ethnic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-chinese">Han Chinese</a> official whose family had long been part of the Manchu Eight Banners. Two other sons the emperor had in mind as heir died young, and in December 1773 he secretly chose Yongyan as his successor. In 1789 he made him Prince Jia of the First Rank.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In October 1795 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qianlong-emperor">Qianlong Emperor</a> announced that he would abdicate, since he thought it disrespectful to rule longer than his grandfather, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kangxi-emperor">Kangxi Emperor</a>. Prince Jia took the throne in February 1796 under the era name Jiaqing, but for three years his father, now emperor emeritus, still made the decisions. After Qianlong died in February 1799 the new emperor took control and prosecuted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heshen">Heshen</a>, his father's corrupt favourite, who was stripped of his titles, lost his property and was ordered to commit suicide.</p><p>The state faced disorder and an empty treasury. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/white-lotus-rebellion">White Lotus Rebellion</a> (1796 to 1804) and the Miao rebellion (1795 to 1806) were put down at a steep fiscal cost. In 1813 the Eight Trigrams, a millenarian Buddhist sect, attacked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/forbidden-city">Forbidden City</a>, intending to kill the emperor on his return from a hunt; the attempt failed. In 1816 the ambassador Lord Amherst came to improve trade with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, but his refusal to perform the kowtow ended the embassy. The emperor refused the Vietnamese ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gia-long">Gia Long</a>'s request to call his country Nam Viet, but agreed to the name Viet Nam. In 1811 a clause added to the legal code made spreading <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> among <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-chinese">Han Chinese</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchus">Manchus</a> punishable by death for Europeans.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>He died on 2 September 1820 at the Rehe summer palace, 230 kilometres north-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>, and was buried in the Chang mausoleum among the Western Qing Tombs. The Draft History of Qing gives no cause of death; some have alleged lightning, others prefer a stroke, as he was very heavy. His second son Mianning succeeded him as the Daoguang Emperor.</p><p>Assessments of his reign are mixed. Some see it as the beginning of the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing dynasty</a>, others as a period of moderate reform. Jonathan Spence thought he relied on rhetoric more than on specific policies. He was a keen scholar of the Confucian classics, with 15,267 poems attributed to him, and had printed compendia of court collections made, which are an important source for Qing court art.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jiaqing Emperor” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The name Viet Nam was granted by the Qing emperor <b>Jiaqing</b> in 1804, after Gia Long asked for Nam Viet, which recalled the ancient kingdom of Nanyue in southern China.</blockquote><a href="/empire/nguyen-dynasty">Nguyen dynasty · At a glance</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Old Summer Palace, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. Died in Chengde Mountain Resort and its outlying temples, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=40.01,116.31&amp;year=1799" aria-label="Old Summer Palace on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7413 732 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Jiaqing Emperor"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7863 1002h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7863 1002h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=40.01,116.31&amp;year=1799">What was here in 1799? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
